"Introduction to special section on Recent Advances in the Study of Optical Variability in the NearSurface and Upper Ocean (https://yang.me.uh.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Dickey_ea_2012_compressed.pdf)\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 39; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view0\",\"lineno\":300}); Total lines: 2459\nL246@P2: [9] This paper concludes by briefly introducing contribu\u0002tions to this special section categorically as follows: (1)\nL247@P2: observational papers based primarily on results from the\nL248@P2: RaDyO program, (2) modeling papers done in conjunction\nL249@P2: with RaDyO, and (3) papers relevant to the theme of the\nL250@P2: special section that were submitted by authors independently\nL251@P2: of RaDyO. Finally, interested readers are directed to a recent\nL252@P2: survey article by Dickey et al. [2011], which briefly provides\nL253@P2: historical, introductory, and summary information relevant\nL254@P2: to this special section.\nL255@P2: 2. Overview of the RaDyO Observational\nL256@P2: Program\nL257@P2: 2.1. Sites of RaDyO Observations\nL258@P2: [10] Fulfillment of the objectives of RaDyO required that a\nL259@P2: wide range of environmental conditions be observed for the\nL260@P2: development of models that would be useful for diverse\nL261@P2: optical, meteorological, and physical situations [Dickey\nL262@P2: et al., 2011]. The first RaDyO experiment was conducted\nL263@P2: using the Scripps Institution of Oceanography (SIO) pier 6–\nL264@P2: 28 January 2008. This work was primarily intended to test\nL265@P2: new instrumentation in a setting with good access to shore\u0002based technical support and to develop integration of the\nL266@P2: diverse methodologies. However, some scientific results\nL267@P2: have been produced from this experiment as well. The sec\u0002ond experiment was conducted in the Santa Barbara Channel\nL268@P2: (SBC) during the period of September 3–25, 2008. This\nL269@P2: setting was selected because it was expected to provide a\nL270@P2: relatively benign wind-wave regime and easy access to shore\nL271@P2: since several new or prototype instrumentation systems were\nL272@P2: being utilized. The third and final RaDyO experiment took\nL273@P2: place south of the Big Island of Hawaii from 24 August\nL274@P2: C00H20 DICKEY ET AL.: INTRODUCTION C00H20\nL275@P2-3: 3 of 39through September 15, 2009. This location was selected\nL276@P3: because of its climatologically high, persistent wind speeds\nL277@P3: and relatively large sea states, its optically clear waters, and\nL278@P3: its open ocean character with good access to the deep-water\nL279@P3: port of Honolulu. The specific area of operations, which lies\nL280@P3: in the North Equatorial Current (NEC), is characterized by\nL281@P3: relatively modest mesoscale eddy activity and minor island\nL282@P3: effects as compared with areas directly in the wind wake of\nL283@P3: the Hawaiian archipelago [e.g., see Dickey et al., 2008]. The\nL284@P3: field experiments are described in more detail below and on\nL285@P3: the Website http://www.opl.ucsb.edu/radyo/.\nL286@P3: 2.2. Platforms\nL287@P3: [11] The multifaceted, multiscale nature of RaDyO pro\u0002blems necessitated a multiple platform, multidisciplinary\nL288@P3: sampling approach as has been discussed in detail in earlier\nL289@P3: references. The primary platforms used for the RaDyO Santa\nL290@P3: Barbara Channel experiment included: R/P FLIP, R/V Kilo\nL291@P3: Moana, an Odyssey autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV)\nL292@P3: [Moline et al., 2012] (Figure 1), a REMUS AUV [Moline\nL293@P3: et al., 2005, 2007, 2010, 2012; Robbins et al., 2006], and\nL294@P3: a surfactant skimmer called the Lil KM (Figure 1). Also, a\nL295@P3: small aircraft equipped with lidar instrumentation made\nL296@P3: measurements over the site [Reineman et al., 2009]. Because\nL297@P3: of anticipated greater sea states, the Odyssey AUV and the\nL298@P3: Lil KM were not utilized and the REMUS AUV was\nL299@P3: deployed on only a limited basis for the Hawaiian experi\u0002ment. In addition, satellite-deployed sensors were utilized to\nL300@P3: obtain wind, sea surface temperature, color, and surface\nL301@P3: roughness (SAR for Santa Barbara experiment only) data.\nL302@P3: The platforms used for the field experiments are briefly\nL303@P3: described below.\nL304@P3: 2.2.1. R/P FLIP\nL305@P3: [12] R/P FLIP (Floating Instrument Platform, Figure 2),\nL306@P3: originally launched on 2 June 1962, was designed to be a\nL307@P3: highly stable platform for oceanographic research in the\nL308@P3: areas of acoustics, seismic waves, ocean waves, and marine\nL309@P3: biology [Fisher and Spiess, 1963]. However, it has since\nL310@P3: been used for a variety of meteorological, air-sea interaction,\nL311@P3: and physical oceanographic experiments [e.g., Siegel and\nL312@P3: Dickey, 1986; Smith et al., 1987; Pinkel et al., 1991;\nL313@P3: Plueddemann et al., 1996; Smith, 1998; Friehe et al., 2001;\nL314@P3: Rainville and Pinkel, 2006] and for optical measurements in\nL315@P3: 1982 [Siegel and Dickey, 1987; Dickey, 1991]. R/P FLIP is\nL316@P3: essentially a large manned spar buoy when oriented verti\u0002cally (note that it is towed horizontally to experiment sites).\nL317@P3: R/P FLIP measures approximately 108 m in length with\nL318@P3: \u000390 m of its hull extending below the water line when on\nL319@P3: station. The diameter of R/P FLIP’s hull is 6.5 m from the 49\nL320@P3: to 91 m depth; the hull tapers to 4 m at the 20 m depth (see\nL321@P3: schematic in Figure 3 of Fisher and Spiess [1963]). This\nL322@P3: design allows R/P FLIP, in free drift mode, to be minimally\nL323@P3: responsive to wave motion (<10% of surface motion, reso\u0002nant period is \u000325 s and thus displaced away from the\nL324@P3: spectral window of greatest wave motion (about 5–18 s)) as\nL325@P3: documented by Fisher and Spiess [1963], Rudnick [1967],\nL326@P3: Rudnick and Hasse [1971], and Smith and Rieder [1997].\nL327@P3: For example, in ten meter waves R/P FLIP’s total vertical\nL328@P3: motion has been recorded to be less than 1 m (see http://\nL329@P3: www.mpl.ucsd.edu/resources/flip.intro.html for a bibliogra\u0002phy and details concerning R/P FLIP).\nL330@P3: [13] For the Santa Barbara Channel RaDyO experiment,\nL331@P3: R/P FLIP (Figure 2a) was moored in place at 34.2053\u0001 N,\nL332@P3: 119.6288\u0001 W in water of 168 m depth using a 3-point\nL333@P3: mooring [e.g., see Bronson, 1971]. Some tilting of R/P FLIP\nL334@P3: was apparent due to currents and winds. These conditions\nL335@P3: were apparently accompanied by a measurable oscillation in\nL336@P3: R/P FLIP’s heading and depth with an approximately 2 min\nL337@P3: period. Instruments were deployed from R/P FLIP’s booms\nL338@P3: to minimize the influence of flow distortion and super\u0002structure interference with measurement devices which were\nL339@P3: either profiled or deployed at or mounted at specific depths.\nL340@P3: Booms with trolleys were used to deploy instrumentation.\nL341@P3: Also, R/P FLIP necessarily discharged wastewaters period\u0002ically and the times of these discharges were noted for pos\u0002sible contamination of optical and chemical data sets. For\nL342@P3: Figure 1. Photographs of the R/V Kilo Moana, Lil KM, R/P FLIP, Odyssey AUV, and REMUS AUV.\nL343@P3: C00H20 DICKEY ET AL.: INTRODUCTION C00H20\nL344@P3-4: 4 of 39reference, R/V Kilo Moana sampled on station about 2 km\nL345@P4: north of R/P FLIP during the SBC experiment except during\nL346@P4: evening treks away from the site to discharge its tanks.\nL347@P4: [14] The Odyssey and REMUS AUVs conducted their\nL348@P4: mapping surveys between R/P FLIP and R/V Kilo Moana as\nL349@P4: well as in their vicinities in patterns described by Moline\nL350@P4: et al. [2012]. Finally, it should be noted that R/P FLIP\nL351@P4: (Figure 2b) was allowed to drift in response to winds and\nL352@P4: currents during the Hawaii RaDyO experiment because\nL353@P4: mooring it in deep water was not feasible for our experiment.\nL354@P4: Furthermore, tilt effects were minimal in this case. The R/V\nL355@P4: Kilo Moana again sampled as close to R/P FLIP as safely\nL356@P4: allowable (generally within 2 km) and the REMUS AUV\nL357@P4: was occasionally used for spatial sampling.\nL358@P4: 2.2.2. R/V Kilo Moana\nL359@P4: [15] The R/V Kilo Moana is a Small Waterplane Area\nL360@P4: Twin Hull (SWATH) vessel of 56.7 m length and with a\nL361@P4: beam of 26.8 and a draft of 7.0–7.6 m [see http://www.soest.\nL362@P4: Figure 2. Instrumentation deployed on R/P FLIP during the (a) Santa Barbara and (b) Hawaii experi\u0002ments in September 2008 and 2009, respectively. (c) R/P FLIP starboard boom during the Hawaii Exper\u0002iment in September 2009. The air-sea flux package, orthogonal scanning laser altimeters, polarimetric and\nL363@P4: infrared cameras are shown in the upper right insert. The acoustic resonators and sonar float [Vagle et al.,\nL364@P4: 2012] are highlighted in the lower insert. (d) Corresponding instrumented R/P FLIP port boom, showing a\nL365@P4: suite of air-sea interaction and subsurface electro-optical sensors used to characterize the influence of\nL366@P4: breaking at the ocean surface on oceanic radiance and imaging (Melville et al., manuscript in preparation,\nL367@P4: 2012): eddy covariance meteorological system, stereo infrared and visible cameras, scanning and single\u0002point laser altimeters, LED downward looking display for imaging through the surface with an underwater\nL368@P4: upward looking color camera at various depths. This subsurface profiler is also equipped with a Nortek\nL369@P4: HR Profiler and ADV for current and turbulence measurements.\nL370@P4: C00H20 DICKEY ET AL.: INTRODUCTION C00H20\nL371@P4-5: 5 of 39hawaii.edu/UMC/KiloMoana.htm for details]. The R/V Kilo\nL372@P5: Moana, which was designed to minimize adverse motions\nL373@P5: via its design and motion compensation system in moderate\nL374@P5: to high sea states, was used for a variety of RaDyO mea\u0002surements. In sea state 5 with significant wave heights of\nL375@P5: 2.5–4.0 m, R/V Kilo Moana ’s maximum pitch is 9.2 deg,\nL376@P5: maximum heave is 8.2 m and maximum roll is 15.2 deg (all\nL377@P5: measured at the vessel’s center of gravity). The instrumen\u0002tation deployed from R/P FLIP and the R/V Kilo Moana is\nL378@P5: introduced below and discussed in more detail in other\nL379@P5: papers in this special section.\nL380@P5: 2.3. Measurement Systems\nL381@P5: [16] Several standard and new technologies were critical\nL382@P5: for meeting the objectives of RaDyO. Some of the novel\nL383@P5: technologies included: (1) optical imaging systems capable\nL384@P5: of sampling at several Hertz and with spatial resolutions\nL385@P5: down to a few mm, (2) new imaging systems with high\nL386@P5: scene dynamic ranges to image both direct and diffuse\nL387@P5: radiance fields, (3) polarigraphic optical measurements, (4)\nL388@P5: underwater radiometric systems consisting of numerous\nL389@P5: radiance and irradiance sensors for fast sampling of wave\u0002induced light fluctuations at 1 kHz frequency, (5) optical\nL390@P5: instruments for measuring absorption and scattering (some\nL391@P5: at multiple scattering angles) at multiple wavelengths in the\nL392@P5: visible spectrum, (6) meteorological instruments for directly\nL393@P5: measuring momentum and heat fluxes, (7) a small surface\nL394@P5: skimming vessel, Lil KM, for collection of microlayer sur\u0002factants and physical and optical measurements in the upper\nL395@P5: 1.7 m of the water column, and (8) two autonomous under\u0002water vehicles for sampling physical and optical variability.\nL396@P5: [17] For convenience, the general descriptions of RaDyO\nL397@P5: measurements are subdivided first by platform and then by\nL398@P5: disciplinary type. Most of the systems were similar for the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroduction to special section on Recent Advances in the Study of Optical Variability in the Near‐Surface and Upper Ocean - Dickey - 2012 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Oceans - Wiley Online Library (https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2012JC007964)\nciteturn19view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view1\",\"lineno\":280}); Total lines: 2060\nL127: cite25†J. Gemmrich ,\nL128: \nL129: J. Gemmrich\nL130: \nL131: Physics and Astronomy, University of Victoria, Victoria, British Columbia, Canada\nL132: \nL133: cite25†Search for more papers by this author L134: \nL135: cite26†P. Gernez ,\nL136: \nL137: P. Gernez\nL138: \nL139: Mer Molécules, Facultés des Sciences et Techniques, Université de Nantes, Nantes, France\nL140: \nL141: cite26†Search for more papers by this author L142: \nL143: cite27†N. Hall-Patch ,\nL144: \nL145: N. Hall-Patch\nL146: \nL147: Institute of Ocean Sciences, Fisheries and Oceans Canada, Sidney, British Columbia, Canada\nL148: \nL149: cite27†Search for more papers by this author L150: \nL151: cite28†B. Holt ,\nL152: B. Holt\nL153: \nL154: Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, California, USA\nL155: \nL156: cite28†Search for more papers by this author L157: \nL158: cite29†S. Jiang ,\nL159: \nL160: S. Jiang\nL161: \nL162: Ocean Physics Laboratory, Department of Geography, University of California, Santa Barbara, California, USA\nL163: \nL164: cite29†Search for more papers by this author L165: \nL166: cite30†C. Jones ,\nL167: \nL168: C. Jones\nL169: \nL170: Earth Research Institute, University of California, Santa Barbara, California, USA\nL171: \nL172: cite30†Search for more papers by this author L173: \nL174: cite31†G. Kattawar ,\nL175: \nL176: G. Kattawar\nL177: Department